{:edb/db-before-t 2503 :edb/db-after-t 2504 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535816 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:59:35.617Z" 13194139535816 true] [17592186216625 1104 #edb/ref 17592186216626 13194139535816 true] [17592186216643 1104 #edb/ref 17592186216647 13194139535816 true] [17592186216953 1100 "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" 13194139535816 true] [17592186216953 1100 "$x^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0$\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" 13194139535816 false] [17592186216953 1145 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}\n$$" 13194139535816 false] [17592186216953 1145 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$" 13194139535816 true] [17592186216954 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" 13194139535816 true] [17592186216954 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n$y''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4$. What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$?" 13194139535816 false] [17592186216954 1145 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW^{′} &= -\\tan (x)W \\\\\n\\frac{dW}{W} &= -\\tan (x)dx \\\\\n∫\\frac{dW}{W} &= ∫ - \\tan (x)dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid W \\mid &= \\ln (\\cos x) + K \\\\\n\\mid W \\mid &= e^{K}\\cos x \\\\\nW &= C\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(0) &= C\\cos (0) \\\\\n4 &= C \\cdot 1 \\\\\nC &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute\n\n$$\nW\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(\\frac{π}{3}) &= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535816 false] [17592186216954 1145 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535816 true] [17592186216955 1100 "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$" 13194139535816 true] [17592186216955 1100 "$y''+4y'+Q(x)y=0$\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3$. Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}$." 13194139535816 false] [17592186216955 1145 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=4\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW^{′} &= -4W \\\\\n\\frac{dW}{W} &= -4dx \\\\\n∫\\frac{dW}{W} &= ∫ - 4dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid W \\mid &= -4x + K 